Since $ker phi nsub G$, the quotient $G \/ ker phi$ is a group. Define $overline(phi): G \/ ker phi -> im phi$ by $g ker phi |-> phi(g)$.

If $g_1 ker phi = g_2 ker phi$, then $g_1 = g_2 k$ for some $k in ker phi$, so $phi(g_1) = phi(g_2)$. The map is a homomorphism because $phi$ is a homomorphism. If $overline(phi)(g ker phi) = e$, then $phi(g) = e$, so $g in ker phi$ and $g ker phi = ker phi$; hence the map is injective. Every element of $im phi$ has the form $phi(g) = overline(phi)(g ker phi)$, so the map is surjective.
